-- ID 510: Property Transactions: Basis — MACRS residential vs. nonresidential recovery period analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Chen purchases a duplex (residential rental) for $275,000 and a small retail building for $390,000 in the same year. Both properties are placed in service on the same date. How does the MACRS classification of residential rental property versus nonresidential real property affect the annual depreciation deductions for each building, and why does the tax code distinguish between them?',
  choices = '["The duplex is depreciated over 27.5 years straight-line, producing larger annual deductions than the retail building''s 39-year straight-line recovery, because Congress uses the shorter period to incentivize housing investment relative to commercial property", "Both buildings are depreciated over 39 years because all real property uses the same recovery period under MACRS regardless of use", "The retail building receives faster depreciation (27.5 years) because commercial property generates more taxable income and needs the larger deduction to offset it", "Both buildings use 15-year 150% declining balance because real property placed in service after 2017 qualifies for accelerated cost recovery"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under MACRS, residential rental property is depreciated over 27.5 years using the straight-line method, while nonresidential real property uses 39 years straight-line. For the duplex, annual depreciation is approximately $10,000 ($275,000 / 27.5), versus approximately $10,000 ($390,000 / 39) for the retail building. Despite similar dollar amounts here due to different costs, the 27.5-year period provides faster cost recovery per dollar invested. Congress set the shorter period for residential property as a policy tool to encourage housing investment and affordability. (B) is wrong because MACRS explicitly distinguishes between residential and nonresidential real property. (C) reverses the recovery periods — residential gets the shorter period, not commercial. (D) is wrong because real property is never eligible for declining balance depreciation under MACRS; it must use straight-line.'
WHERE id = 510;

-- ID 512: Property Transactions: Basis — MACRS asset classification impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Nakamura, a sole proprietor, purchases a delivery van for $48,000 and office furniture costing $12,500 for her consulting business in January 2025. Her CPA classifies the van as 5-year MACRS property and the furniture as 7-year property. How does this classification difference affect Nakamura''s depreciation deductions in the first three years, and what principle determines why vehicles and furniture receive different recovery periods?',
  choices = '["The van generates larger first-year deductions because 5-year property uses 200% declining balance with a shorter recovery period, producing faster cost recovery than the 7-year furniture — asset classes are based on IRS estimates of useful economic life, with vehicles wearing out faster than furniture", "No difference — both assets use straight-line depreciation and produce identical annual deductions because all personal property under MACRS uses the same method", "The furniture produces larger deductions because 7-year property uses a more accelerated method than 5-year property to compensate for the longer recovery period", "Both assets produce identical deductions because MACRS assigns the same recovery period to all business personal property regardless of asset class"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): MACRS classifies assets by Revenue Procedure guidelines tied to estimated useful lives. Automobiles and light trucks fall in the 5-year class, while office furniture falls in the 7-year class. Both use 200% declining balance switching to straight-line, but the shorter 5-year period produces larger annual depreciation percentages. In year 1 under the half-year convention, 5-year property yields 20% versus 14.29% for 7-year property. The IRS assigns vehicles a shorter recovery period because they typically have shorter economic useful lives due to wear, mileage, and obsolescence compared to furniture. (B) is wrong because MACRS personal property uses declining balance, not straight-line (straight-line applies to real property). (C) reverses the relationship — shorter recovery periods produce faster cost recovery, not longer ones. (D) is wrong because MACRS explicitly categorizes assets into different recovery period classes.'
WHERE id = 512;

-- ID 515: Property Transactions: Basis — Nonresidential real property recovery period change impact
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Morrison, a commercial landlord, placed a warehouse in service in April 1993 and a second warehouse in service in June 1993. How does the mid-May 1993 threshold date affect the recovery period for each warehouse, and what was the consequence of Congress extending the nonresidential recovery period from 31.5 to 39 years?',
  choices = '["The April warehouse uses a 31.5-year recovery period while the June warehouse uses 39 years, because Congress changed the rule effective May 13, 1993 — the longer period reduces annual depreciation deductions, meaning the June warehouse generates less tax shelter per year", "Both warehouses use 39 years because the change applied retroactively to all nonresidential property regardless of when placed in service", "Both warehouses use 27.5 years because all real property placed in service in 1993 was classified as residential", "The April warehouse uses 40 years under ADS while the June warehouse uses 39 years under GDS, because property placed in service before the change must use the alternative depreciation system"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Nonresidential real property placed in service after May 13, 1993 uses a 39-year straight-line recovery period, while property placed in service on or before that date uses 31.5 years. Morrison''s April warehouse qualifies for the shorter 31.5-year period, yielding approximately 3.17% annual depreciation, while the June warehouse uses 39 years at approximately 2.56%. The practical consequence is lower annual deductions for the newer property — Congress extended the period to reduce the tax subsidy for commercial real estate and raise revenue. (B) is wrong because the change was prospective, not retroactive — property already in service retained its original recovery period. (C) is wrong because 27.5 years applies only to residential rental property, not commercial warehouses. (D) is wrong because the change involved the GDS recovery period, not a switch to ADS; pre-May 1993 property stayed under GDS at 31.5 years.'
WHERE id = 515;

-- ID 516: Property Transactions: Basis — Half-year convention first-year rate derivation
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Petrov acquires 7-year MACRS equipment for $100,000 in March, while Santiago acquires identical equipment for $100,000 in November of the same year. Both use the half-year convention with 200% declining balance. How does the half-year convention affect the first-year depreciation rate for each taxpayer, and why does the convention produce a 14.29% rate rather than the full 28.57% declining balance rate?',
  choices = '["Petrov gets a higher first-year rate because March placement captures more months, while Santiago gets a lower rate for November placement — the convention is month-sensitive", "Both receive the same 14.29% first-year rate because the half-year convention treats all property as placed in service at the midpoint of the year regardless of actual date, effectively halving the 28.57% full-year 200% DB rate to prevent taxpayers from front-loading deductions through year-end purchases", "Both receive 28.57% because the half-year convention does not reduce the first-year rate — it only affects the final year of the recovery period", "Petrov receives 14.29% and Santiago receives 7.14% because the half-year convention pro-rates based on the quarter of placement"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The 200% declining balance rate for 7-year property is 2/7 = 28.57%. The half-year convention treats all property as placed in service at the midpoint of the tax year, regardless of the actual date, so only half the full-year rate applies in year 1: 28.57% × 0.5 = 14.29%. Both Petrov (March) and Santiago (November) receive the identical 14.29% rate. The convention''s purpose is to simplify depreciation and prevent gaming — without it, taxpayers would rush purchases into January to maximize first-year deductions. (A) is wrong because the half-year convention is not month-sensitive; it treats all placements identically. (C) is wrong because the convention explicitly reduces the first-year deduction, not just the final year. (D) describes a quarterly convention, not the half-year convention — the mid-quarter convention is a separate rule that applies only when more than 40% of property is placed in service in the fourth quarter.'
WHERE id = 516;

