-- ID 538: Individual Taxation: Income — At-risk rules limitation impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Webber invests $80,000 cash and borrows $120,000 on a nonrecourse loan (not secured by real property) to acquire a partnership interest. The partnership generates a $150,000 loss in Year 1. How does the at-risk limitation under Section 465 affect Webber''s deductible loss, and why does the rule treat nonrecourse debt differently from recourse debt?',
  choices = '["Webber may deduct the full $150,000 because the at-risk rules only apply to individuals earning over $250,000 AGI", "Webber may deduct only $80,000 because nonrecourse debt where the taxpayer bears no personal economic risk is excluded from the at-risk amount — the rule prevents taxpayers from generating tax deductions using borrowed money they are not personally obligated to repay", "Webber may deduct $120,000 because the at-risk amount equals the nonrecourse portion only, excluding the cash investment", "Webber may deduct $200,000 because all borrowings are included in the at-risk amount regardless of recourse status"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under Section 465, a taxpayer''s deductible loss is limited to the amount at risk, which includes cash contributed and amounts borrowed for which the taxpayer has personal liability (recourse debt). Nonrecourse debt — where the lender''s only remedy is to seize the collateral — is generally excluded from the at-risk amount (with a limited exception for qualified nonrecourse financing on real property). Webber''s at-risk amount is $80,000 (cash only), so only $80,000 of the $150,000 loss is deductible. The remaining $70,000 is suspended until additional at-risk basis is created. The policy rationale is that taxpayers should not shelter income with losses backed by debt they might never actually pay. (A) is wrong because at-risk rules apply regardless of AGI level. (C) reverses the inclusion — cash is at risk, nonrecourse debt is not. (D) is wrong because the entire purpose of Section 465 is to distinguish recourse from nonrecourse debt.'
WHERE id = 538;

-- ID 539: Individual Taxation: Income — Passive activity loss compartmentalization analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Torres earns $200,000 in wages, $30,000 in dividend income, and reports a $45,000 loss from a limited partnership in which she does not materially participate. How does the passive activity loss rule under Section 469 affect Torres''s ability to use the partnership loss, and what is the consequence of the rule''s income compartmentalization approach?',
  choices = '["Torres may offset the $45,000 loss against her wages because W-2 income is considered active income from personal services", "Torres may deduct $30,000 of the loss against dividends and suspend $15,000, because portfolio income is treated as passive income", "Torres may not deduct any of the $45,000 loss in the current year because passive losses can only offset passive income — the compartmentalization prevents taxpayers from using tax shelter losses to reduce tax on wages and portfolio income, with suspended losses carried forward until passive income arises or the activity is fully disposed of", "Torres may deduct the full $45,000 because limited partnership losses are exempt from the passive activity rules after the TCJA"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Section 469 creates three compartments of income: active (wages, business income with material participation), portfolio (dividends, interest, capital gains), and passive (activities without material participation). Passive losses can only offset passive income. Torres has no passive income, so the entire $45,000 loss is suspended and carried forward. The compartmentalization prevents the tax shelter strategy that prompted the 1986 legislation — taxpayers investing in loss-generating partnerships to shelter wages and investment income. Suspended losses are freed upon disposition of the entire activity in a taxable transaction. (A) is wrong because wages are active income, which is precisely the category passive losses cannot offset. (B) is wrong because dividends are portfolio income, not passive — Section 469 explicitly excludes portfolio income from the passive category. (D) is wrong because the TCJA did not repeal or modify the passive activity loss rules.'
WHERE id = 539;

-- ID 542: Individual Taxation: Income — Real estate professional exception analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Martinez works 900 hours in her real estate brokerage and 700 hours in material participation in her five rental properties during 2025. Her colleague, Pham, works 800 hours in a real estate development firm and 600 hours in his three rental properties. Both have rental losses. How does the real estate professional qualification under Section 469(c)(7) affect each person''s ability to deduct rental losses differently?',
  choices = '["Both qualify as real estate professionals because they each work more than 750 hours in real property trades, so both can deduct all rental losses against any income", "Martinez qualifies because she meets both tests — more than 750 hours in real property trades or businesses and more than half her personal services in those activities — so her rental losses are not automatically passive. Pham may fail if his 800 real estate hours do not constitute more than half his total personal services for the year", "Neither qualifies because the real estate professional exception was eliminated for tax years after 2023", "Only Pham qualifies because the 800-hour requirement is higher than the 750-hour threshold, and Martinez''s brokerage does not count as a real property trade"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Section 469(c)(7) requires two tests: (1) more than 750 hours of services in real property trades or businesses, and (2) more than half of the taxpayer''s total personal services must be in real property trades or businesses. Martinez meets both — 900 hours exceeds 750, and her 900 real estate hours exceed her other personal service hours (making up the majority). Pham''s 800 hours exceed 750, but if he has substantial hours in other non-real-estate activities, his real estate hours may not constitute more than half his total services. The two-test structure ensures the exception applies only to genuine real estate professionals, not taxpayers who merely invest some time in real estate. (A) is wrong because it ignores the majority-of-services test — the 750-hour test alone is insufficient. (C) is wrong because the real estate professional exception remains in effect. (D) is wrong because real estate brokerage is explicitly listed as a real property trade or business under the statute.'
WHERE id = 542;

-- ID 551: Individual Taxation: Income — Passive activity disposition and loss release analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Kowalski has $120,000 in suspended passive losses from a rental property. In 2025, he sells the property in a fully taxable transaction for a $30,000 gain. What is the effect of the complete disposition on Kowalski''s suspended passive losses, and how would the result differ if he had instead gifted the property to his daughter?',
  choices = '["In a taxable sale, all $120,000 of suspended losses are released and deductible against any income because a complete disposition in a taxable transaction triggers full loss recognition; in a gift, the suspended losses are added to the recipient''s basis rather than being released to the donor", "In both cases the $120,000 remains suspended because passive losses can never be deducted regardless of disposition", "In a taxable sale only $30,000 of suspended losses are released to offset the gain, with $90,000 permanently lost; in a gift the full $120,000 is released", "In both cases the $120,000 is immediately deductible because any disposition, whether taxable or not, releases all suspended passive losses"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under Section 469(g), when a taxpayer disposes of an entire interest in a passive activity in a fully taxable transaction, all accumulated suspended passive losses are released and may offset any type of income (active, portfolio, or passive). Kowalski''s $120,000 in suspended losses would be fully deductible, netting against the $30,000 gain and producing a net $90,000 deduction against other income. However, in a gift, the suspended losses are not released to the donor — instead, they increase the donee''s basis in the property under Section 469(j)(6). This distinction is critical for planning because a gift permanently denies the donor the ability to use those suspended losses. (B) is wrong because the complete disposition rule specifically allows deduction of suspended losses. (C) is wrong because the full amount is released in a taxable sale, not just the gain amount. (D) is wrong because gifts and other nontaxable transfers do not trigger loss release — only fully taxable dispositions qualify.'
WHERE id = 551;

