-- ID 61: Circular 230 — Authorized practice analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'An enrolled agent (EA) who passed the Special Enrollment Examination represents a client in an IRS audit. A bookkeeper with 15 years of experience at the same firm requests permission to represent a different client before the IRS Appeals Office. How does Circular 230 distinguish between the two practitioners'' authority, and why does the distinction exist?',
  choices = '["Both have unlimited practice rights because any tax professional with sufficient experience may represent clients before the IRS", "The EA has unlimited practice rights before the IRS because passing the SEE demonstrates competency tested by the Treasury Department, while the bookkeeper has no practice rights regardless of experience — Circular 230 limits authorized practitioners to CPAs, attorneys, and enrolled agents to protect taxpayers from unqualified representation", "The bookkeeper has greater authority because practical experience outweighs examination credentials under Circular 230", "Neither may represent clients because only attorneys are authorized to practice before the IRS"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under Treasury Circular 230, only three categories of practitioners have unlimited rights to practice before the IRS: enrolled agents (who pass the three-part SEE), CPAs, and attorneys. The EA''s practice rights derive from demonstrating competency through the government-administered examination. A bookkeeper, regardless of years of experience, is not authorized to represent clients before the IRS (except in limited circumstances as an unenrolled preparer for returns they personally prepared, only before revenue agents). The gatekeeping purpose is to ensure that taxpayers receive competent representation from individuals who have demonstrated knowledge of tax law. (A) is wrong because experience alone does not confer practice rights — Circular 230 requires specific credentials. (C) reverses the framework — examination credentials are required, not overridden by experience. (D) is wrong because CPAs and enrolled agents also have unlimited practice rights, not just attorneys.'
WHERE id = 61;

-- ID 198: Circular 230 — Sanctions analysis and comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is found to have violated Circular 230 by recklessly advising clients on aggressive tax positions. The Office of Professional Responsibility (OPR) is considering sanctions. How do the available OPR sanctions under Circular 230 differ from criminal penalties, and what determines which sanction level the OPR selects?',
  choices = '["OPR can impose criminal imprisonment because violations of Circular 230 are federal crimes", "OPR can seize the practitioner''s personal assets to compensate affected clients, similar to a civil judgment", "OPR can impose censure, suspension, or disbarment from practice before the IRS — these are administrative sanctions that restrict the practitioner''s ability to represent clients, unlike criminal penalties which require prosecution by the DOJ. The severity depends on the nature of the violation, whether it was willful, and the practitioner''s prior disciplinary history", "OPR can revoke the practitioner''s CPA license, effectively ending their ability to practice accounting in any capacity"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The OPR''s authority under Circular 230 is administrative, not criminal. Available sanctions are censure (public reprimand), suspension (temporary loss of practice rights), and disbarment (permanent revocation of rights to practice before the IRS). These are distinct from criminal penalties, which require separate prosecution by the Department of Justice. The OPR selects the sanction level based on severity factors including whether the conduct was willful or reckless, the harm to clients or the tax system, and the practitioner''s disciplinary history. Censure is appropriate for less severe violations, while disbarment is reserved for the most egregious conduct. (A) is wrong because OPR has no criminal jurisdiction — imprisonment requires DOJ prosecution. (B) is wrong because OPR cannot order asset seizure or client restitution; those are judicial remedies. (D) is wrong because CPA licensure is controlled by state boards of accountancy, not the OPR — disbarment from IRS practice does not automatically revoke a CPA license.'
WHERE id = 198;

-- ID 199: Circular 230 — Unconscionable fee factor analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Practitioner Allen charges a new client $25,000 to prepare a straightforward individual return with W-2 income and a standard deduction. The client challenges the fee. Under Circular 230, what factors would determine whether Allen''s fee is unconscionable, and how does this analysis differ from a simple market-rate comparison?',
  choices = '["The fee is automatically unconscionable because it exceeds $5,000, which is the Circular 230 maximum for individual returns", "The unconscionability analysis considers the practitioner''s experience, reputation, and ability, the complexity and novelty of the work, the customary charges for similar services, and the results obtained — this multi-factor approach differs from a simple market comparison because a high fee may be justified by extraordinary expertise or complexity, and a seemingly reasonable fee may still be unconscionable if the work was negligible", "Only the client''s ability to pay determines unconscionability — if the client can afford the fee, it cannot be unconscionable regardless of the work performed", "Only the practitioner''s office location and overhead costs are relevant, because Circular 230 requires fees to be proportional to operating expenses"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Circular 230 does not set dollar limits on fees. Instead, it uses a multi-factor test evaluating the practitioner''s experience, reputation, and ability; the novelty and difficulty of the questions involved; the skill required; the customary charge for similar services in the locality; the amount involved and results obtained; and the time limitations imposed. This holistic analysis means that a $25,000 fee for a simple W-2 return is likely unconscionable because the complexity is minimal and the fee vastly exceeds customary charges, but the same fee for a complex international tax matter may be entirely reasonable. The analysis is context-dependent, not formula-based. (A) is wrong because Circular 230 imposes no dollar cap on fees. (C) is wrong because the client''s financial capacity is not a factor — unconscionability is measured by the relationship between the fee and the services rendered. (D) is wrong because overhead costs are not among the specified factors; the analysis focuses on the nature of the work and the practitioner''s qualifications.'
WHERE id = 199;

-- ID 487: Circular 230 — Section 6694(b) penalty severity analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Practitioner Reynolds willfully understates a client''s tax liability, and the IRS asserts the Section 6694(b) penalty. Reynolds earned $4,000 from preparing that return. A colleague, Dunn, commits a non-willful unreasonable position violation under Section 6694(a) and earned $3,000 from her return. How do the penalty calculations differ between the two preparers, and what policy rationale explains the difference in severity?',
  choices = '["Reynolds owes $5,000 (the greater of $5,000 or 75% of $4,000 = $3,000) under Section 6694(b), while Dunn owes $3,000 (the greater of $1,000 or 50% of $3,000 = $1,500) under Section 6694(a) — the willful penalty is substantially more severe because it punishes intentional misconduct rather than mere negligence, creating a deterrent proportional to culpability", "Both owe identical $1,000 penalties because preparer penalties are flat amounts regardless of the nature of the violation", "Reynolds owes $250 per return and Dunn owes $500 per return, because willful violations receive lower penalties to encourage voluntary disclosure", "Reynolds owes $1,000 and Dunn owes $5,000, because the more severe penalty applies to non-willful violations as a strict liability measure"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 6694(b) penalizes willful understatement at the greater of $5,000 or 75% of income from the return. For Reynolds: 75% × $4,000 = $3,000, so the penalty is $5,000 (the greater amount). Section 6694(a) penalizes unreasonable positions at the greater of $1,000 or 50% of income. For Dunn: 50% × $3,000 = $1,500, so the penalty is $1,500 (the greater amount). The graduated structure reflects Congress''s intent to punish intentional misconduct far more harshly than negligent errors. The income-based component ensures that high-earning preparers face proportionally larger penalties, preventing them from treating penalties as a cost of doing business. (B) is wrong because both penalties use a greater-of formula, not flat amounts. (C) reverses the severity — willful violations carry harsher penalties, not lighter ones. (D) also reverses the severity and mischaracterizes the standard — Section 6694(a) is not strict liability.'
WHERE id = 487;

-- ID 488: Circular 230 — Statute of limitations extension trigger analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Taxpayer Griffin reports $200,000 of gross income on his return but fails to include $60,000 of cash receipts from a side business. Taxpayer Wells files a return with an incorrect filing status but reports all income accurately. How does the 25% omission threshold affect the statute of limitations differently for each taxpayer, and why does Congress treat income omissions more seriously than other errors?',
  choices = '["Griffin''s statute extends to six years because the $60,000 omission exceeds 25% of the $200,000 reported gross income, triggering the extended assessment period under Section 6501(e); Wells retains the standard three-year statute because incorrect filing status is not an income omission — Congress treats substantial income omissions more seriously because they directly reduce the tax base and are difficult for the IRS to detect without extended audit time", "Both have a six-year statute because any error on a return automatically extends the limitations period", "Griffin retains three years because the omission must exceed 50% to trigger extension, and $60,000 is only 30% of $200,000", "Wells gets six years because filing status errors are considered more serious than income omissions under the Code"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 6501(e) extends the assessment period to six years when a taxpayer omits gross income exceeding 25% of the amount stated on the return. Griffin reported $200,000, and the $60,000 omission is 30% of the reported amount (exceeding the 25% threshold). Wells''s filing status error, while potentially affecting tax liability, is not an omission of gross income and does not trigger the six-year rule. Congress designed the extended period specifically for income omissions because they are inherently harder to detect — the IRS must independently discover income the taxpayer never reported, which requires more investigative time than correcting computational or classification errors visible on the return. (B) is wrong because only specific triggers (income omission >25%, fraud, non-filing) extend the statute; routine errors do not. (C) is wrong because the threshold is 25%, not 50%. (D) is wrong because filing status errors do not trigger the extended statute — only substantial income omissions qualify.'
WHERE id = 488;

