-- ID 229: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits — Child tax credit phase-out impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The Andersons (MFJ) have three qualifying children under 17 and AGI of $400,000. The Garcias (MFJ) also have three qualifying children under 17 but have AGI of $450,000. How does the phase-out mechanism affect the child tax credit differently for each family, and what is the effect on the credit amount per child?',
  choices = '["The Andersons receive the full $6,000 credit ($2,000 × 3 children) because their AGI is at the $400,000 MFJ threshold, while the Garcias'' credit is reduced by $2,500 ($50 per $1,000 of AGI above $400,000 × $50,000 excess) to $3,500 — the phase-out targets the benefit away from higher-income households while preserving it fully for those at or below the threshold", "Both families receive $6,000 because the phase-out does not begin until $500,000 for MFJ filers", "Neither family receives any credit because the phase-out completely eliminates the credit at $400,000 AGI", "The Garcias receive more credit because higher AGI increases the refundable portion of the child tax credit"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The child tax credit provides $2,000 per qualifying child under 17, producing a maximum of $6,000 for three children. The phase-out for MFJ filers begins at $400,000 AGI and reduces the credit by $50 for every $1,000 (or fraction thereof) of AGI above the threshold. The Andersons'' AGI equals the threshold exactly, so they receive the full $6,000. The Garcias'' AGI exceeds the threshold by $50,000, reducing their credit by $2,500 ($50 × 50), leaving $3,500. The phase-out mechanism ensures the credit provides the greatest benefit to families below the income threshold while gradually reducing it for higher earners. (B) is wrong because the MFJ phase-out begins at $400,000, not $500,000. (C) is wrong because the phase-out is gradual, not a cliff — the credit is reduced incrementally rather than eliminated entirely at the threshold. (D) is wrong because higher AGI reduces the credit; the refundable portion (additional child tax credit) is subject to its own limitations based on earned income.'
WHERE id = 229;

-- ID 1256: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits — Standard deduction comparison across filing statuses
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'For 2026, the standard deduction for MFJ is $30,000, for HOH is $22,500, and for Single is $15,000. Thompson and her spouse are considering whether to file MFJ or MFS (which uses the Single amount). How does the choice between MFJ and MFS affect their combined standard deduction, and why might a couple still choose MFS despite the smaller deduction?',
  choices = '["MFJ provides a $30,000 standard deduction while MFS would give each spouse $15,000 (totaling $30,000), making the deduction identical — couples choose MFS to avoid joint liability for each other''s tax obligations, separate income-driven student loan payments, or qualify for deductions with lower AGI thresholds", "MFJ always produces lower total tax because the standard deduction is exactly double the Single amount, eliminating any possible benefit from filing separately", "MFS provides a larger combined deduction because each spouse can claim the full MFJ amount independently", "There is no difference — all filing statuses receive the same standard deduction amount"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): While the MFJ standard deduction ($30,000) equals the sum of two MFS deductions ($15,000 + $15,000 = $30,000), the standard deduction is only one factor. MFS may be advantageous when one spouse has significant medical expenses (the 7.5% AGI floor is lower with separate AGI), when separating liability for a spouse''s questionable tax positions, or when income-based repayment plans for student loans use individual AGI. The MFJ filing status provides the largest single standard deduction, but the tax consequences extend beyond just the deduction to include rate brackets, credit eligibility, and liability exposure. (B) is wrong because MFJ does not always produce lower total tax — situations involving medical deduction thresholds, separate liability concerns, or income-driven loan payments can make MFS beneficial despite the same combined deduction. (C) is wrong because each MFS spouse gets only the Single-equivalent amount, not the full MFJ amount. (D) is wrong because the standard deduction differs significantly by filing status.'
WHERE id = 1256;

-- ID 1257: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits — Filing status determination date impact
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Baker marries on December 30, 2025. His colleague, Reeves, finalizes a divorce on December 28, 2025. Both had been filing Single for the first 11 months of the year. How does the December 31 filing status determination rule affect each taxpayer''s filing status for the entire year of 2025, and what is the consequence for their tax liability?',
  choices = '["Baker files as MFJ (or MFS) for all of 2025 because marital status on December 31 controls the entire year, while Reeves files as Single (or possibly HOH if qualifying) because the divorce was final before year-end — this single-date rule means two days of marriage gives Baker access to MFJ brackets and deductions for the full year, while Reeves loses MFJ eligibility despite being married for most of the year", "Baker files Single for January through December 29 and MFJ for December 30-31, because filing status is determined monthly", "Both file as Single because filing status is determined on January 1 of the tax year", "Reeves files MFJ because the marriage lasted for the majority of 2025 regardless of the December divorce"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Federal tax law uses a single determination date — December 31 (or the date of death if the taxpayer dies during the year). Filing status is not prorated. Baker, married on December 30, is considered married for the entire year and may file MFJ with a spouse, gaining access to wider tax brackets and the larger standard deduction. Reeves, divorced on December 28, is unmarried on December 31 and cannot file MFJ despite 362 days of marriage. This bright-line rule creates planning opportunities: a December marriage captures MFJ benefits for the whole year, while a December divorce eliminates them. (B) is wrong because filing status is never prorated by month or day — it is determined once for the entire year. (C) is wrong because the determination date is December 31, not January 1. (D) is wrong because the majority-of-year concept does not apply to filing status — only the December 31 snapshot matters.'
WHERE id = 1257;

-- ID 1258: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits — Qualifying child test distinction analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Yang''s 16-year-old son earns $8,000 from a summer job. Yang''s neighbor, Ortiz, claims his 22-year-old nephew who lives with him and earns $3,500. Why does the gross income test NOT apply to Yang''s son as a qualifying child, and how does this differ from the qualifying relative dependency test that Ortiz must use for the nephew?',
  choices = '["The qualifying child test uses age, relationship, residency, and support tests but deliberately excludes a gross income test — this allows parents to claim working teenagers regardless of earnings. The qualifying relative test requires gross income below the exemption amount ($5,050 for 2025) because it covers a broader range of dependents where income is a better proxy for financial independence", "Both must pass the gross income test, but Yang''s son is exempt because he is under 18", "Neither must pass a gross income test because all dependency tests were eliminated by the TCJA", "The gross income test applies only to qualifying children, not qualifying relatives, so Yang must verify his son''s income while Ortiz does not"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The qualifying child test requires four conditions: relationship, age (under 19, or under 24 if full-time student), residency (more than half the year), and that the child not provide more than half of their own support. Notably, there is no gross income test — Congress recognized that teenagers earning summer income remain dependents of their parents. Ortiz''s 22-year-old nephew does not qualify as a qualifying child (over 19 and presumably not a full-time student), so Ortiz must use the qualifying relative test, which imposes a gross income limit ($5,050 for 2025). Since the nephew earns $3,500, he passes. The structural difference reflects Congress''s view that minor children are presumptively dependent regardless of earnings, while other potential dependents must demonstrate financial dependence through low income. (B) is wrong because the qualifying child test has no gross income test at any age. (C) is wrong because the TCJA suspended the personal exemption amount but retained the dependency tests for credits and HOH status. (D) reverses which category requires the gross income test.'
WHERE id = 1258;

-- ID 1259: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits — HOH qualification factor analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Sarah is unmarried and maintains a home with her 14-year-old daughter who earns $3,000 from a part-time job. Sarah''s brother, Tom, is unmarried and maintains a home with his 20-year-old son who is not a student and earns $12,000. Why does Sarah qualify for Head of Household while Tom likely does not, and how does the daughter''s $3,000 income affect Sarah''s qualification?',
  choices = '["Sarah qualifies for HOH because her daughter is a qualifying child (under 19, lives with Sarah more than half the year) — the daughter''s $3,000 income is irrelevant because the qualifying child test has no gross income requirement. Tom likely fails because his 20-year-old non-student son does not meet the qualifying child age test, and as a qualifying relative the son''s $12,000 income exceeds the gross income limit, eliminating dependency", "Sarah qualifies only because her daughter earns under $5,050, while Tom fails because his son earns over $5,050", "Neither qualifies for HOH because unmarried taxpayers with dependents must file Single", "Both qualify for HOH because maintaining a household with any family member is sufficient regardless of dependency status"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): HOH requires the taxpayer to be unmarried, pay more than half the cost of maintaining a home, and have a qualifying person living there. Sarah''s daughter meets the qualifying child test (relationship, age under 19, residency over half the year), and because the qualifying child test has no gross income requirement, the daughter''s $3,000 earnings do not affect HOH eligibility. Tom''s son is 20 and not a student, so he fails the qualifying child age test (must be under 19). Tom must use the qualifying relative test, which requires the son''s gross income to be below $5,050 (2025). At $12,000, the son fails, and Tom cannot claim him as a dependent — eliminating HOH eligibility. (B) is partially wrong because it misapplies the gross income test to qualifying children; the daughter''s income is irrelevant, not compared to a threshold. (C) is wrong because HOH is specifically designed for unmarried taxpayers with dependents. (D) is wrong because the household member must be a qualifying dependent, not just any family member.'
WHERE id = 1259;

