-- ID 82: C Corporations — Flat rate impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Prior to the TCJA, C corporations paid graduated rates from 15% to 35%. After the TCJA, the rate became a flat 21%. How does the shift from graduated to flat rate affect a small corporation with $50,000 of taxable income differently than a large corporation with $10,000,000, and what policy tradeoff does the flat rate represent?',
  choices = '["The small corporation pays more under the flat 21% rate ($10,500) than it would have under the old graduated system (15% on the first $50,000 = $7,500), while the large corporation pays significantly less (21% vs. effective rate approaching 35%) — the flat rate represents a tradeoff of simplicity and corporate competitiveness against the progressive principle that larger earners should bear proportionally higher rates", "Both corporations pay less under the flat rate because 21% is lower than every bracket in the old graduated system", "The flat rate only affects corporations with income over $1 million; small corporations are exempt", "Both corporations pay the same effective rate under both systems because the graduated rates averaged to 21%"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under the pre-TCJA graduated structure, the first $50,000 of corporate income was taxed at 15%, producing $7,500 in tax. The flat 21% rate produces $10,500 on $50,000 — a $3,000 increase for the small corporation. Conversely, the large corporation''s $10,000,000 would have been taxed at rates up to 35% (effective rate near 34%), producing approximately $3,400,000 — far more than the $2,100,000 under the flat 21%. The policy tradeoff is that the flat rate simplifies compliance and improves international competitiveness but eliminates the progressive structure that charged larger corporations more. (B) is wrong because the old 15% bracket was lower than 21%, meaning small corporations actually paid less under the graduated system. (C) is wrong because the flat 21% rate applies to all C corporations regardless of size. (D) is wrong because the graduated rates did not average to 21% — they ranged from 15% to 35% with the effective rate varying by income level.'
WHERE id = 82;

-- ID 84: C Corporations — Accumulated earnings tax trigger analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Beacon Corp. has $500,000 of accumulated earnings and its board can document reasonable business needs of $250,000 for expansion. The accumulated earnings credit is $250,000. Rival firm Crestline Corp. has $500,000 of accumulated earnings but can document only $50,000 in reasonable business needs. How does the ability to justify business needs affect each corporation''s exposure to the accumulated earnings tax, and why does this penalty exist?',
  choices = '["Beacon faces no accumulated earnings tax because its documented business needs ($250,000) equal or exceed the accumulated earnings credit ($250,000), leaving no excess accumulation. Crestline faces the tax on the excess because it can only justify $50,000 of the $250,000 beyond the credit — the penalty exists to prevent shareholders from using the corporate structure to defer individual income tax by retaining earnings rather than distributing dividends", "Both face the accumulated earnings tax because any accumulation over $100,000 is automatically penalized regardless of business justification", "Neither faces the tax because the accumulated earnings tax was repealed by the TCJA when the corporate rate dropped to 21%", "Only Crestline faces the tax, but only because its earnings exceed $1 million — the $250,000 credit eliminates all liability for corporations under that threshold"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The accumulated earnings tax under Section 531 applies to corporations that accumulate earnings beyond the reasonable needs of the business. Every corporation receives a minimum credit of $250,000 (or $150,000 for certain personal service corporations). Beacon can justify the full amount above the credit through documented expansion needs, so there is no taxable excess. Crestline can justify only $50,000 beyond the credit, leaving $200,000 ($500,000 − $250,000 credit − $50,000 documented needs) subject to the 20% accumulated earnings tax ($40,000). The penalty exists to prevent closely-held corporations from retaining earnings to avoid shareholder-level dividend taxation — without it, shareholders could defer individual tax indefinitely by leaving profits in the corporation. (B) is wrong because the tax applies to accumulations beyond reasonable needs, not a fixed dollar threshold. (C) is wrong because the accumulated earnings tax was not repealed — it remains in the Code. (D) is wrong because the $250,000 credit is a minimum floor, not a threshold based on total earnings.'
WHERE id = 84;

-- ID 230: C Corporations — Property distribution amount analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Nexus Corp. distributes equipment with an FMV of $80,000 and an adjusted basis of $50,000 to shareholder Park as a dividend. In a separate transaction, Nexus distributes a vehicle with an FMV of $30,000 and an adjusted basis of $45,000 to shareholder Lee. How does the tax treatment differ between the two distributions, and why does the Code use FMV rather than basis to measure the distribution amount?',
  choices = '["Park''s distribution amount is $80,000 (FMV) and Nexus recognizes $30,000 gain; Lee''s distribution amount is $30,000 (FMV) but Nexus cannot recognize the $15,000 loss — FMV is used to measure distributions because it represents the actual economic benefit received by the shareholder, while the loss disallowance on depreciated property prevents corporations from manufacturing deductible losses through selective distributions", "Both distributions are measured at adjusted basis ($50,000 and $45,000) because the shareholder takes a carryover basis in distributed property", "Both distributions are measured at FMV and both allow gain or loss recognition by the corporation, with no distinction between appreciated and depreciated property", "Park''s distribution is $50,000 (basis) and Lee''s is $45,000 (basis) because corporations never recognize gain or loss on distributions"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under Section 311, when a corporation distributes appreciated property, it recognizes gain as if the property were sold at FMV ($80,000 − $50,000 = $30,000 gain for Nexus on Park''s distribution). The distribution amount to the shareholder is measured at FMV ($80,000) because that represents the economic benefit received. However, Section 311(a) prohibits loss recognition on distributions of depreciated property — Nexus cannot deduct the $15,000 loss on Lee''s vehicle ($30,000 FMV − $45,000 basis). This asymmetry prevents corporations from selectively distributing depreciated assets to shareholders to generate tax losses while distributing appreciated assets through sales. Lee''s distribution is still measured at $30,000 FMV. (B) is wrong because distribution amounts are measured at FMV, not basis — the shareholder receives FMV-based treatment. (C) is wrong because loss recognition is specifically disallowed on distributions, creating asymmetric treatment. (D) is wrong because gain is recognized on appreciated property distributions; only losses are disallowed.'
WHERE id = 230;

-- ID 572: C Corporations — 501(c)(3) qualification factor analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Hope Foundation operates a charitable hospital and pays its CEO a $2 million salary (consistent with market rates for hospital executives). Justice League Inc. operates an educational nonprofit but distributes 10% of net earnings to its board members as annual bonuses unrelated to services. How does the private inurement prohibition under Section 501(c)(3) affect each organization''s tax-exempt status differently?',
  choices = '["Hope Foundation''s $2 million CEO salary does not violate the private inurement prohibition because it represents reasonable compensation for services rendered, which is permitted. Justice League''s distribution of earnings to board members as non-service bonuses constitutes private inurement because net earnings are benefiting insiders rather than serving exempt purposes — this distinction is why the IRS evaluates the reasonableness of compensation rather than imposing dollar caps", "Both organizations lose exempt status because any payment exceeding $500,000 to an individual constitutes automatic private inurement", "Neither organization has a problem because the private inurement prohibition only applies to political organizations under 501(c)(4)", "Hope Foundation violates the prohibition because no nonprofit executive should earn over $500,000, while Justice League is fine because board bonuses are standard practice"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 501(c)(3) requires that no part of net earnings inure to the benefit of private shareholders or individuals. Reasonable compensation for services is not inurement — it is a necessary operating expense. Hope Foundation''s $2 million salary, if consistent with comparable hospital executive compensation, passes the reasonableness test. Justice League''s non-service bonuses represent net earnings flowing to insiders for personal benefit, which is the core definition of inurement and can result in loss of exempt status. The IRS uses a facts-and-circumstances test for compensation reasonableness, comparing to market rates for similar positions, rather than imposing arbitrary caps. (B) is wrong because there is no fixed dollar threshold for inurement — the test is reasonableness relative to services rendered. (C) is wrong because private inurement is a fundamental requirement for all 501(c)(3) organizations, not limited to 501(c)(4). (D) reverses the analysis and fabricates a salary cap that does not exist in the Code.'
WHERE id = 572;

