-- ID 64: Business Law: Contracts — Statute of Frauds scope analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Henderson orally agrees to sell a parcel of land to Mitchell for $50,000. Separately, Torres orally agrees to paint Chen''s house for $8,000, completing the job within two weeks. When Henderson attempts to enforce the oral land sale, Mitchell raises the Statute of Frauds. Torres''s client, Chen, attempts the same defense. How does the Statute of Frauds affect these two contracts differently, and what policy purpose does the distinction serve?',
  choices = '["Henderson cannot enforce the oral land sale because real property transfers must be in writing under the Statute of Frauds — the risk of fraud in land transactions is high due to their value and complexity. Torres can enforce the oral painting contract because it is for services capable of completion within one year and does not fall within any Statute of Frauds category, regardless of the dollar amount", "Both contracts are unenforceable because any oral contract exceeding $5,000 falls within the Statute of Frauds", "Both contracts are enforceable because the Statute of Frauds was abolished in most jurisdictions", "The painting contract requires writing because it exceeds $500, while the land sale is enforceable orally because both parties verbally agreed"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The Statute of Frauds requires certain categories of contracts to be in writing: transfers of real property interests, contracts not performable within one year, contracts for goods $500+ (UCC), suretyship promises, and contracts in consideration of marriage. Henderson''s land sale falls squarely within the real property category and is unenforceable without writing. Torres''s painting contract, although worth $8,000, is performable within two weeks (well under one year) and involves services (not goods), so it does not fall within any Statute of Frauds category. The Statute targets transactions where the risk of perjury or faulty memory is greatest — land transactions are complex, high-value, and occur infrequently, justifying the writing requirement. (B) is wrong because the Statute of Frauds is category-based, not dollar-based (except for UCC goods contracts at $500+). (C) is wrong because the Statute of Frauds remains in effect in all U.S. jurisdictions. (D) reverses the analysis — the land sale requires writing while the service contract does not, and the $500 UCC threshold applies only to goods.'
WHERE id = 64;

-- ID 65: Business Law: Contracts — Mailbox rule timing analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'On Monday, Allen mails an offer to Baker. On Wednesday, Allen mails a revocation. On Thursday, before receiving the revocation, Baker mails an acceptance. Allen argues the revocation was sent before the acceptance. How does the mailbox rule resolve this conflict, and what would happen differently if Baker had mailed a rejection on Wednesday and then mailed an acceptance on Thursday?',
  choices = '["The acceptance on Thursday creates a binding contract because under the mailbox rule, acceptance is effective upon dispatch (mailing), while revocation is effective only upon receipt — since Baker mailed the acceptance before receiving the revocation, the contract formed Thursday. If Baker had mailed a rejection Wednesday followed by an acceptance Thursday, the mailbox rule would not protect the acceptance because the first response dispatched was a rejection, making whichever communication arrives first controlling", "No contract was formed because the revocation was mailed before the acceptance, and the mailbox rule applies to both revocations and acceptances equally", "The contract formed on Wednesday when Allen mailed the revocation, because revocations take priority over acceptances under the UCC", "The contract formed on Friday when Baker receives the revocation, because all contract communications are effective upon receipt"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The mailbox rule creates an asymmetry: acceptances are effective upon dispatch (when mailed), while revocations are effective only upon receipt. Baker''s Thursday acceptance was effective the moment it was mailed, before the Wednesday revocation was received. The contract formed Thursday. The overtaking acceptance scenario (rejection mailed first, acceptance mailed second) is the key analytical extension — the mailbox rule does not apply when the offeree sends a rejection first, because the offeror should be able to rely on the first communication received. In that case, whichever communication arrives first controls. (B) is wrong because the mailbox rule specifically treats acceptances and revocations differently — acceptances are effective on dispatch, revocations on receipt. (C) is wrong because revocations never take priority based on mailing date; they must be received. (D) is wrong because the common law mailbox rule makes acceptances effective upon dispatch, not upon receipt.'
WHERE id = 65;

-- ID 201: Business Law: Contracts — Consideration validity analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Dawson promises Henderson $5,000 if Henderson will refrain from filing a legitimate lawsuit against Dawson. Separately, Martinez promises to pay her neighbor $1,000 in gratitude for helping her move three months ago. Why does Henderson''s forbearance constitute valid consideration while Martinez''s promise does not, and how does this distinction affect contract enforceability?',
  choices = '["Henderson''s forbearance is valid consideration because surrendering or promising to forbear from exercising a legal right constitutes a bargained-for legal detriment — Henderson is giving up something of value (the right to sue). Martinez''s promise is unenforceable because past consideration is not valid consideration; the neighbor''s help occurred before the promise, so there was no bargained-for exchange at the time of the promise", "Both promises lack valid consideration because money must flow both ways for a contract to be enforceable", "Both promises constitute valid consideration because any promise of payment creates a binding obligation", "Martinez''s promise is enforceable because moral obligation is sufficient consideration, while Henderson''s forbearance fails because not suing is not a legal detriment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Valid consideration requires a bargained-for exchange of legal value at the time of the agreement. Henderson''s forbearance from exercising a legal right (the right to file a lawsuit) is a present legal detriment that supports Dawson''s promise — Henderson is giving up something of value in exchange for the payment. Martinez''s promise fails because the neighbor''s moving help was completed before the promise was made. Past consideration is not valid consideration because there was no bargain at the time the benefit was conferred — the neighbor did not act in exchange for a promise of payment. The distinction is temporal: consideration must be part of the present exchange, not gratitude for a past act. (B) is wrong because consideration does not require money to flow both ways — a promise to forbear or perform an act is sufficient. (C) is wrong because past consideration and gratuitous promises are not enforceable. (D) reverses both conclusions — moral obligation generally fails while forbearance of a legal right is well-established valid consideration.'
WHERE id = 201;

-- ID 202: Business Law: Contracts — Perfect tender rule analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'FastShip delivers 1,000 widgets to Greenfield Manufacturing, but 15 widgets have minor cosmetic scratches that do not affect functionality. Under a separate contract, BulkCo delivers 500 units of industrial chemicals to Apex Corp, but the delivery arrives two days late. How does the UCC perfect tender rule affect each buyer''s rights, and what limitation exists on strict rejection?',
  choices = '["Under the perfect tender rule, both Greenfield and Apex may reject the entire shipment because any deviation from contract specifications — whether in quality or delivery timing — constitutes a failure of perfect tender. However, the seller has a right to cure if time for performance remains, and in installment contracts the substantial impairment test replaces perfect tender, meaning the rule''s strictness is tempered by practical limitations that prevent opportunistic rejection of immaterial defects", "Greenfield must accept because the scratches are minor, but Apex can reject because late delivery is always a material breach regardless of circumstances", "Neither buyer can reject because the UCC requires buyers to accept goods if they are substantially conforming", "Both must accept and can only sue for damages, because the perfect tender rule was replaced by the substantial performance doctrine for all UCC contracts"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The UCC perfect tender rule (Section 2-601) gives buyers the right to reject goods (or any commercial unit) if they fail to conform to the contract in any respect — including cosmetic defects and late delivery. Both Greenfield and Apex have rejection rights. However, the rule has important limitations: the seller has a right to cure defects if the time for performance has not expired (Section 2-508), and installment contracts use a substantial impairment test rather than perfect tender (Section 2-612). These limitations prevent buyers from using trivial nonconformities as a pretext to escape contracts when market conditions change. (B) is wrong because the perfect tender rule applies to quality defects, not just delivery timing. (C) is wrong because the UCC uses perfect tender for single-delivery contracts, not substantial conformity. (D) is wrong because the perfect tender rule remains the standard for single-delivery UCC contracts; substantial performance is a common law doctrine that does not apply to goods contracts.'
WHERE id = 202;

-- ID 496: Business Law: Contracts — Buyer remedy selection analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'DataTech Corp receives a shipment of 500 computer monitors from ScreenPro. Upon inspection, 100 monitors have dead pixels, 200 have minor packaging damage but work perfectly, and 200 are fully conforming. What remedies are available to DataTech under UCC Article 2, and how does the ability to accept part and reject part affect DataTech''s strategic decision-making compared to an all-or-nothing approach?',
  choices = '["DataTech must accept all 500 monitors and can only recover damages for the 100 defective units through a separate lawsuit", "DataTech must reject all 500 monitors because partial acceptance is not permitted under the UCC — the perfect tender rule requires total rejection or total acceptance", "DataTech has no remedy because it inspected the goods before rejecting, which constitutes implied acceptance", "DataTech may reject the 100 dead-pixel monitors, accept the 400 functioning monitors (including the 200 with packaging damage), and recover damages for the nonconforming units — this partial acceptance option allows DataTech to retain usable goods while rejecting defective ones, which is strategically superior to total rejection because it avoids supply disruption for the 400 working units"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under UCC Section 2-601, the buyer may accept the whole, reject the whole, or accept any commercial unit(s) and reject the rest. This flexibility is the key analytical point — DataTech can strategically accept the 400 working monitors (even those with packaging damage, since they function properly) while rejecting the 100 with dead pixels. This is superior to total rejection because DataTech avoids a supply gap for the 80% of monitors that meet operational needs, while still preserving rights to damages and cover for the rejected units. Under Section 2-714, DataTech can recover the difference between the value of conforming goods and the value received. (A) is wrong because the buyer is never required to accept all goods; the UCC provides three options. (B) is wrong because partial acceptance by commercial unit is explicitly permitted under Section 2-601. (C) is wrong because inspection does not constitute acceptance — the UCC provides a reasonable time to inspect before acceptance occurs.'
WHERE id = 496;

