-- ID 1266: Tax-Exempt Organizations — Political activity prohibition vs. lobbying analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Green Future Foundation, a 501(c)(3) environmental charity, spends 5% of its budget lobbying for clean water legislation. The same organization''s executive director publicly endorses a mayoral candidate at a fundraising event. How does the tax code distinguish between these two activities, and why is one absolutely prohibited while the other is permitted within limits?',
  choices = '["Both activities are equally prohibited for 501(c)(3) organizations and will result in immediate loss of exempt status", "Both activities are permitted without limitation because the First Amendment protects nonprofit speech", "Lobbying for legislation is permitted in insubstantial amounts (or under the Section 501(h) expenditure test) because influencing policy relates to the organization''s exempt purpose, while campaign intervention for a specific candidate is absolutely prohibited because it injects tax-subsidized resources into partisan elections — the distinction reflects Congress''s judgment that policy advocacy serves the public interest while partisan campaign activity does not", "Campaign intervention is permitted if it represents less than 5% of activities, while lobbying is absolutely prohibited regardless of amount"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Section 501(c)(3) draws a sharp line between lobbying and political campaign activity. Lobbying (attempting to influence legislation) is permitted as long as it does not constitute a substantial part of the organization''s activities, or under the Section 501(h) election which sets specific expenditure limits. Campaign intervention — participating or intervening in any political campaign for or against a candidate — is absolutely prohibited with no de minimis exception. One instance can trigger loss of exempt status and a 10% excise tax. The policy rationale is that donors receive tax deductions for charitable contributions, and Congress determined that funneling tax-subsidized dollars into partisan campaigns is fundamentally inconsistent with charitable status. (A) is wrong because lobbying is permitted within limits; only campaign activity is absolutely prohibited. (B) is wrong because the tax code does impose restrictions on exempt organizations despite First Amendment protections. (D) reverses the rules — campaign activity has no safe harbor percentage, while lobbying is the activity permitted within limits.'
WHERE id = 1266;

-- ID 1267: Tax-Exempt Organizations — UBIT income type exclusion analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Community Arts Foundation, a 501(c)(3), earns $50,000 in dividends from its investment portfolio, $80,000 from a gift shop selling art supplies to the public (staffed by paid employees), and $30,000 from a thrift store staffed entirely by volunteers. How does the UBIT framework analyze each income stream differently, and what principle determines whether passive investment income is excluded?',
  choices = '["All three income streams are excluded from UBIT because 501(c)(3) organizations are exempt from all taxation", "The dividends ($50,000) are excluded from UBIT because passive investment income (dividends, interest, rents, royalties) is specifically excluded under Section 512(b) as it does not involve active business competition with taxable entities. The gift shop income ($80,000) is likely UBIT because it is a regularly carried on trade or business not substantially related to the exempt purpose. The thrift store income ($30,000) is excluded because the volunteer labor exception applies when substantially all work is performed by unpaid volunteers", "All three streams are subject to UBIT because they all generate revenue from commercial activities", "Only the dividends are subject to UBIT because investment income competes with for-profit investment firms"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): UBIT analysis applies a three-part test: the income must come from (1) a trade or business, (2) regularly carried on, (3) not substantially related to the exempt purpose. However, Section 512(b) excludes passive investment income (dividends, interest, certain rents, royalties) from UBIT because passively holding investments does not constitute operating a competing business. The gift shop selling art supplies to the general public through paid employees is likely a regularly carried-on unrelated business. The thrift store qualifies for the volunteer exception under Section 513(a)(1), which excludes businesses where substantially all labor is performed without compensation. Each exclusion serves a distinct policy purpose: passive income exclusions protect the investment function, while the volunteer exception recognizes that volunteer-run operations do not unfairly compete with taxable businesses. (A) is wrong because exempt organizations are subject to UBIT on unrelated business income. (C) is wrong because specific statutory exclusions remove qualifying income from UBIT. (D) reverses the treatment — investment income is excluded, not included.'
WHERE id = 1267;

-- ID 1268: Tax-Exempt Organizations — Specific deduction purpose analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A 501(c)(3) organization has $8,000 of gross unrelated business income and $6,500 in directly connected expenses. Without the specific deduction, the UBTI would be $1,500. With the $1,000 specific deduction, the UBTI is $500. Why does the Code provide this $1,000 specific deduction against unrelated business taxable income, and how does it affect small versus large exempt organizations differently?',
  choices = '["The $1,000 specific deduction functions as a de minimis threshold that effectively exempts organizations with small amounts of net unrelated business income from filing and paying UBIT — for the organization in this example, it reduces the taxable amount from $1,500 to $500. For large organizations with millions in UBIT, the $1,000 is negligible, meaning the deduction primarily benefits smaller organizations by reducing compliance burden on trivial amounts of unrelated income", "The $1,000 deduction replaces all other deductions — organizations must choose between the specific deduction and itemizing expenses", "The deduction is $1,000 per unrelated activity, so organizations with multiple unrelated businesses receive $1,000 for each", "The $1,000 deduction only applies to organizations with gross receipts under $25,000 and has no effect on larger organizations"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 512(b)(12) provides a flat $1,000 specific deduction against unrelated business taxable income. Its primary purpose is administrative simplification — organizations with small amounts of net unrelated income receive a meaningful reduction that may eliminate or substantially reduce their UBIT liability. For the example organization, the $1,000 reduces taxable income by 67% (from $1,500 to $500). For a large university with $5 million in UBIT, the $1,000 is a 0.02% reduction — essentially meaningless. The deduction thus operates as a progressive benefit that disproportionately helps smaller organizations. (B) is wrong because the specific deduction is in addition to directly connected expenses, not a replacement. (C) is wrong because each organization receives only one $1,000 specific deduction regardless of the number of unrelated activities. (D) is wrong because the deduction is available to all organizations subject to UBIT regardless of their gross receipts.'
WHERE id = 1268;

-- ID 1269: Tax-Exempt Organizations — Self-dealing excise tax analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A private foundation''s founder sells office equipment to the foundation at fair market value. The foundation''s attorney, who serves as a board member, approves the transaction believing FMV makes it permissible. How does Section 4941 treat this transaction, and why does the self-dealing prohibition apply even when the price is fair?',
  choices = '["The founder faces a 10% initial excise tax on the amount involved as the self-dealer, and the attorney faces a 5% tax as a foundation manager who knowingly participated — Section 4941 imposes a per se prohibition on sales between disqualified persons and private foundations regardless of fairness, because allowing FMV exceptions would require constant valuation disputes and create opportunities for disguised self-dealing that undermine public trust in the charitable sector", "No tax consequence applies because the transaction was at fair market value, which satisfies the arm''s-length standard for private foundations", "Only the founder is penalized; foundation managers face no liability for approving self-dealing transactions", "The foundation loses its exempt status immediately upon any self-dealing transaction, with no intermediate excise tax"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 4941 treats sales (and other specified transactions) between disqualified persons (including founders) and private foundations as acts of self-dealing regardless of whether the price is fair. The initial tax is 10% of the amount involved, imposed on the self-dealer (the founder). A foundation manager (the attorney) who knowingly participates faces a 5% tax (up to $20,000). The per se rule — no FMV exception — exists because Congress determined that case-by-case valuation analysis would be unworkable and easily manipulated. By prohibiting all such transactions, the rule eliminates the incentive for insiders to structure deals with foundations and ensures foundation assets are used exclusively for charitable purposes. (B) is wrong because Section 4941 is a per se rule — fair market value is irrelevant and does not provide an exception. (C) is wrong because knowing foundation managers face their own 5% excise tax. (D) is wrong because the first consequence is an excise tax, not loss of exemption; repeated or uncorrected self-dealing may eventually lead to termination, but that is not the initial remedy.'
WHERE id = 1269;

