-- ID 223: Individual Taxation: Deductions — SALT cap impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The Nguyen family (MFJ) pays $12,000 in state income tax and $8,000 in property tax ($20,000 total). Their neighbors, the Petersons (MFJ), pay $4,000 in state income tax and $5,000 in property tax ($9,000 total). How does the $10,000 SALT cap introduced by the TCJA affect each family differently, and what was the policy rationale for capping this deduction?',
  choices = '["The Nguyens can only deduct $10,000 despite paying $20,000 in SALT, losing a $10,000 deduction compared to pre-TCJA rules. The Petersons deduct their full $9,000 because they are under the cap. The cap disproportionately affects taxpayers in high-tax states by limiting the federal subsidy for state and local taxation — Congress used the revenue from the cap to offset the cost of lower rates elsewhere in the TCJA", "Both families deduct their full SALT payments because the $10,000 cap only applies to single filers", "The Nguyens deduct $20,000 and the Petersons deduct $9,000, because the SALT cap was overturned by the courts in 2020", "Both families are limited to $5,000 each because the MFJ SALT cap is $5,000 per spouse"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The TCJA capped the SALT deduction at $10,000 for all filers ($5,000 for MFS). The Nguyens'' $20,000 in combined state income and property taxes exceeds the cap by $10,000, meaning they lose a significant deduction they would have fully claimed under prior law. The Petersons'' $9,000 falls below the cap, so they are unaffected. The cap''s impact falls most heavily on taxpayers in states with high income and property taxes (New York, California, New Jersey), effectively reducing the federal subsidy that previously made high state taxes less burdensome. The revenue generated from the cap helped finance the overall rate reductions in the TCJA. (B) is wrong because the $10,000 cap applies to all filing statuses (except MFS at $5,000). (C) is wrong because the SALT cap remains in effect; legal challenges were unsuccessful. (D) is wrong because the MFJ cap is $10,000, not $5,000 per spouse.'
WHERE id = 223;

-- ID 224: Individual Taxation: Deductions — Self-employed health insurance deduction analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Rivera, a self-employed consultant, earns $90,000 net profit and pays $12,000 in health insurance premiums. Her employee, Walsh, pays the same $12,000 through an employer-sponsored plan where the employer pays half. How does the tax treatment of health insurance premiums differ between Rivera and Walsh, and why is Rivera''s deduction classified as above-the-line rather than as an itemized deduction?',
  choices = '["Rivera deducts the full $12,000 as an above-the-line deduction (reducing AGI), which is available regardless of whether she itemizes and reduces AGI for purposes of other AGI-sensitive calculations. Walsh''s employer-paid portion ($6,000) is excluded from income entirely, and Walsh''s $6,000 share may be pre-tax through a cafeteria plan. The above-the-line treatment for self-employed individuals approximates the exclusion employees receive, achieving rough parity between employment arrangements", "Rivera may only deduct premiums exceeding 7.5% of AGI as a medical expense on Schedule A, receiving no special treatment for self-employment", "Rivera deducts $6,000 (50%) because the self-employed deduction is limited to half of premiums paid", "Both receive identical treatment because the TCJA equalized health insurance deductions for all taxpayers"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Self-employed taxpayers may deduct 100% of health insurance premiums as an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1, subject to the limitation that the deduction cannot exceed net self-employment income. Rivera''s $12,000 deduction reduces her AGI from $90,000 to $78,000, affecting calculations for medical expense thresholds, passive loss phase-outs, and other AGI-sensitive provisions. Congress placed this deduction above-the-line to approximate the treatment employees receive — when an employer pays health premiums, those amounts are excluded from the employee''s gross income entirely (an even better result). Without the above-the-line deduction, self-employed taxpayers would need to itemize and clear the 7.5% AGI floor, creating a significant disparity. (B) is wrong because Congress specifically created the above-the-line deduction to avoid forcing self-employed individuals onto Schedule A. (C) is wrong because the deduction is 100%, not 50%, subject to the net profit limitation. (D) is wrong because the treatment differs between self-employed and employed individuals — employees typically get exclusion through employer plans.'
WHERE id = 224;

-- ID 225: Individual Taxation: Deductions — Charitable contribution holding period impact analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Park donates publicly traded stock with FMV of $20,000 and a basis of $5,000 to a qualified public charity. She has held the stock for 3 years. Her colleague, Kim, donates identical stock (same FMV and basis) held for only 8 months. How does the holding period affect the charitable deduction amount for each donor, and what policy purpose does this distinction serve?',
  choices = '["Park deducts $20,000 (FMV) because long-term capital gain property donated to a public charity is deductible at FMV, preserving the full economic benefit of the donation. Kim deducts only $5,000 (basis) because short-term capital gain property must be reduced to basis — this prevents taxpayers from inflating deductions by donating recently acquired appreciated assets that would have generated ordinary income or short-term gain if sold", "Both deduct $20,000 because all charitable contributions of stock are deductible at FMV regardless of holding period", "Park deducts $5,000 (basis) and Kim deducts $20,000 (FMV), because longer holding periods reduce the deduction", "Both deduct $15,000 (FMV minus basis) because charitable deductions are always limited to the gain portion of donated property"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 170 provides that long-term capital gain property (held more than one year) donated to a public charity is deductible at FMV, subject to a 30% AGI limitation. Park''s 3-year holding period qualifies, so she deducts the full $20,000. Kim''s 8-month holding period means the stock is short-term capital gain property, which must be reduced by the amount that would have been short-term capital gain if sold — reducing the deduction to basis ($5,000). The policy purpose is to prevent a planning strategy where taxpayers buy stock, wait for appreciation, and quickly donate to claim a deduction on unrealized short-term gain that would otherwise be taxed at ordinary rates. The long-term requirement ensures the appreciation represents genuine economic growth rather than speculative short-term trading. (B) is wrong because the holding period directly affects whether the FMV or basis deduction applies. (C) reverses the treatment — longer holding periods enable the larger FMV deduction. (D) is wrong because the deduction is either FMV or basis, not the gain amount.'
WHERE id = 225;

-- ID 226: Individual Taxation: Deductions — QBI deduction limitation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Porter, a self-employed architect (specified service business) has $300,000 QBI and $400,000 taxable income (MFJ). Martinez, a self-employed manufacturer (non-service business) has $300,000 QBI and $400,000 taxable income (MFJ). Both claim the Section 199A deduction. How does the specified service business classification affect each taxpayer''s QBI deduction, and why does the Code treat these business types differently?',
  choices = '["Both receive a $60,000 deduction (20% × $300,000) because the specified service business limitation only applies above the $364,200 MFJ threshold, and at $400,000 both are in the phase-out range but Porter''s deduction begins to phase out while Martinez''s is unaffected — the distinction exists because Congress viewed service businesses (law, accounting, health, consulting) as deriving income primarily from personal skill rather than capital investment, and sought to limit the deduction''s benefit for high-earning service professionals", "Both receive $60,000 because Section 199A provides the same 20% deduction to all pass-through businesses regardless of type", "Porter receives $0 because all specified service businesses are completely excluded from the QBI deduction", "Martinez receives $0 because manufacturing businesses do not generate qualified business income"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 199A provides a 20% deduction on QBI, but specified service trades or businesses (SSTBs) — including health, law, accounting, consulting, athletics, financial services, and performing arts — face a phase-out at higher income levels. For MFJ filers, the phase-out begins at $364,200 and is complete at $464,200 (2024 amounts, indexed). At $400,000, Porter is within the phase-out range and receives a reduced deduction. Martinez''s manufacturing business is not an SSTB, so the service business phase-out does not apply (though the W-2 wage/property limitations may). Congress distinguished SSTBs because these businesses derive income primarily from the owner''s personal services — similar to wage income — rather than from capital assets, employees, or business infrastructure that the deduction was designed to incentivize. (B) is wrong because the SSTB classification creates different treatment at higher income levels. (C) is wrong because SSTBs are not completely excluded — they receive the full deduction below the threshold and a partial deduction in the phase-out range. (D) is wrong because manufacturing is a qualifying trade or business for Section 199A.'
WHERE id = 226;

