-- ID 553: Individual Taxation: Credits — AOTC vs. LLC structural comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Brennan is a freshman in college (year 1 of 4), and her mother claims the American Opportunity Tax Credit. Patel is a graduate student in his sixth year of higher education, and he claims the Lifetime Learning Credit. How do the structural differences between the AOTC and LLC affect each taxpayer''s benefit, and why did Congress design two education credits with different eligibility rules?',
  choices = '["Brennan benefits from the AOTC''s larger maximum credit ($2,500), partial refundability (40% = $1,000 refundable), and the requirement of being in the first four years of postsecondary education. Patel benefits from the LLC because it has no limit on the number of years claimed, making it available for graduate and continuing education. Congress created two credits to serve different populations — the AOTC incentivizes completing undergraduate degrees with a more generous but time-limited benefit, while the LLC supports lifelong learning with a smaller but perpetually available nonrefundable credit", "Both credits offer the same maximum amount and refundability; the only difference is the income phase-out ranges", "The AOTC is available for unlimited years while the LLC is limited to 4 years, making the LLC more restrictive for graduate students", "The LLC provides a larger credit ($4,000 maximum) than the AOTC ($2,500 maximum), making it the preferred choice for all students"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The AOTC provides up to $2,500 per student for the first four years of postsecondary education, with 40% ($1,000) refundable — meaning low-income students benefit even with no tax liability. The LLC provides up to $2,000 per return (20% of $10,000 in expenses) with no limit on the number of years, but it is entirely nonrefundable. The two-credit structure reflects distinct policy goals: the AOTC''s generosity and refundability incentivize degree completion for undergraduate students, while the LLC''s unlimited duration supports graduate education, professional development, and lifelong learning. Patel could not use the AOTC (past year 4), but the LLC ensures he still receives a tax benefit. (B) is wrong because the credits differ significantly in maximum amount, refundability, and year limitations. (C) reverses the year limitations — the AOTC is limited to 4 years and the LLC has no year limit. (D) is wrong because the AOTC maximum ($2,500) exceeds the LLC maximum ($2,000).'
WHERE id = 553;

-- ID 554: Individual Taxation: Credits — Foreign tax credit limitation formula analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Taylor earns $50,000 from foreign sources and $150,000 from U.S. sources ($200,000 worldwide). U.S. tax before credits is $44,000. Taylor paid $15,000 in foreign taxes. How does the foreign tax credit limitation formula prevent Taylor from using foreign taxes to reduce U.S. tax on domestic income, and what happens to any foreign taxes that exceed the limitation?',
  choices = '["The limitation is $11,000 ($44,000 × $50,000/$200,000), restricting the credit to the proportion of U.S. tax attributable to foreign-source income. Although Taylor paid $15,000 in foreign taxes, only $11,000 may be credited currently — the remaining $4,000 excess may be carried back one year or forward ten years. The formula prevents foreign taxes from offsetting U.S. tax on the $150,000 of domestic income, preserving U.S. taxing jurisdiction over domestic-source earnings", "Taylor credits the full $15,000 because the foreign tax credit has no limitation — all foreign taxes paid are fully creditable", "The limitation is $44,000 because the credit can never exceed total U.S. tax, but no proportional formula applies", "Taylor receives $0 credit because the foreign tax credit is only available when foreign taxes exceed $20,000"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The foreign tax credit limitation under Section 904 is calculated as: U.S. tax × (foreign-source taxable income / worldwide taxable income). For Taylor: $44,000 × ($50,000 / $200,000) = $11,000. This means Taylor can only credit $11,000 of the $15,000 paid, with the $4,000 excess available for carryback (1 year) or carryforward (10 years). The limitation''s purpose is to prevent double non-taxation — without it, a taxpayer with high foreign taxes could eliminate U.S. tax on domestic income entirely. The formula ensures the credit only offsets U.S. tax proportional to foreign earnings. (B) is wrong because the credit is subject to the Section 904 limitation based on the ratio of foreign to worldwide income. (C) is wrong because the proportional formula is the primary limitation; the overall U.S. tax cap is a secondary ceiling. (D) is wrong because there is no minimum threshold — any amount of creditable foreign taxes may be claimed up to the limitation.'
WHERE id = 554;

-- ID 555: Individual Taxation: Credits — AMT preference item analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Hoffman earns $60,000 in tax-exempt interest from private activity municipal bonds issued in 2020 and $40,000 in tax-exempt interest from general obligation municipal bonds. For AMT purposes, how are these two types of tax-exempt interest treated differently, and what policy rationale explains why private activity bond interest is an AMT preference item?',
  choices = '["Private activity bond interest ($60,000) must be added back to taxable income for AMT purposes because Congress determined that using tax-exempt financing for private business purposes (airports, housing, industrial development) provides a double benefit — tax-free income to the bondholder plus subsidized financing for private entities. General obligation bond interest ($40,000) remains fully exempt from AMT because it finances core governmental functions. This distinction ensures that investors who benefit from private-purpose tax exemptions bear a minimum tax burden", "Both types of interest are AMT preference items because all municipal bond interest is subject to AMT", "Neither is an AMT preference item because the TCJA eliminated all AMT adjustments for individual taxpayers", "General obligation bond interest is the preference item, while private activity bond interest is exempt from AMT"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Tax-exempt interest on private activity bonds issued after August 7, 1986 is an AMT preference item under Section 57(a)(5). Hoffman must add $60,000 to alternative minimum taxable income (AMTI). General obligation bonds are not preference items and remain fully exempt from both regular tax and AMT. The policy rationale is that private activity bonds finance projects that benefit private entities (airports, hospitals, industrial facilities, multifamily housing), and the tax exemption already provides a subsidy by lowering borrowing costs. Congress viewed the additional benefit of AMT-free income for bondholders of private-purpose bonds as excessive, while preserving full exemption for bonds that finance core governmental services like schools, roads, and public safety. (B) is wrong because general obligation bond interest is specifically excluded from AMT preferences. (C) is wrong because the TCJA reformed but did not eliminate the individual AMT or its preference items. (D) reverses the treatment — private activity bonds are the preference item, not general obligation bonds.'
WHERE id = 555;

