-- ID 74: Like-Kind Exchanges — Qualifying property type analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ramirez wants to exchange a rental warehouse for a retail shopping center. Her business partner, Cho, wants to exchange 1,000 shares of stock in a real estate company for a different portfolio of real estate stocks. Both believe they qualify for Section 1031 like-kind exchange treatment. How does Section 1031 distinguish between these two exchanges, and what is the consequence for each taxpayer?',
  choices = '["Ramirez qualifies for Section 1031 because both the warehouse and shopping center are real property held for investment or used in a trade or business — the exchange of one type of real estate for another satisfies the like-kind requirement. Cho does not qualify because stocks, bonds, and other securities are explicitly excluded from Section 1031 regardless of the underlying assets they represent — Cho must recognize any gain or loss immediately", "Both qualify because real estate stocks represent indirect ownership of real property, which satisfies the like-kind requirement", "Neither qualifies because Section 1031 was fully repealed by the TCJA in 2017", "Ramirez does not qualify because a warehouse and a shopping center are different property types, while Cho qualifies because stock-for-stock exchanges are like-kind"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Section 1031 allows tax-deferred exchanges of real property held for productive use in a trade or business or for investment. After the TCJA, only real property qualifies (personal property was excluded starting in 2018). Ramirez''s warehouse-for-shopping-center exchange qualifies because both are real property held for business/investment purposes — the like-kind standard for real estate is broad, allowing exchanges between different types of real property. Cho''s stock exchange fails because Section 1031(a)(2) explicitly excludes stocks, bonds, notes, and other securities, even if the underlying companies own real estate. The exclusion exists because securities are liquid assets with established markets, making deferral unnecessary and potentially abusive. (B) is wrong because indirect ownership through securities does not satisfy Section 1031; the taxpayer must exchange real property directly. (C) is wrong because the TCJA narrowed but did not repeal Section 1031 — it remains available for real property. (D) reverses the qualifications — different types of real estate are like-kind, while securities are excluded.'
WHERE id = 74;

-- ID 531: Like-Kind Exchanges — Installment sale gross profit percentage analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Harper sells property for $200,000 (basis $120,000) with $50,000 down and $150,000 in installments over 3 years. Mercer sells different property for $200,000 (basis $160,000) with the same payment terms. How does the difference in basis affect each seller''s gross profit percentage and annual gain recognition under the installment method, and what planning advantage does the installment method provide?',
  choices = '["Harper''s gross profit percentage is 40% ($80,000 gain / $200,000 price) and Mercer''s is 20% ($40,000 gain / $200,000 price) — each payment received is treated as part return of basis and part gain in these proportions. Harper recognizes $20,000 gain on the $50,000 down payment (40%) while Mercer recognizes $10,000 (20%). The installment method''s advantage is matching gain recognition with cash receipt, preventing taxation on gain before the seller has received sufficient cash to pay the tax", "Both sellers have a 40% gross profit percentage because the percentage is based on the selling price, not the gain", "Harper''s percentage is 60% and Mercer''s is 80%, because the gross profit percentage equals basis divided by selling price", "Both must recognize the full gain in the year of sale because the installment method only applies when no down payment is received"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The installment method under Section 453 calculates a gross profit percentage (total gain / contract price) that applies to each payment received. Harper: ($200,000 − $120,000) / $200,000 = 40%. Mercer: ($200,000 − $160,000) / $200,000 = 20%. When Harper receives the $50,000 down payment, 40% ($20,000) is gain and 60% ($30,000) is return of basis. Mercer recognizes only $10,000 gain on the same down payment. The lower basis produces a higher percentage, meaning more of each dollar received is taxable. The installment method''s key advantage is liquidity matching — sellers are not forced to pay tax on gain before receiving the cash, which is especially valuable when the seller finances the purchase over multiple years. (B) is wrong because the percentage depends on gain relative to price, which varies with basis. (C) confuses the formula — it uses basis/price instead of gain/price. (D) is wrong because the installment method applies regardless of whether a down payment is received; it is the default method for qualifying sales.'
WHERE id = 531;

-- ID 533: Like-Kind Exchanges — Involuntary conversion partial reinvestment analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Henderson''s warehouse is destroyed by fire. Insurance proceeds are $500,000 and the adjusted basis is $350,000. Henderson purchases a replacement warehouse for $480,000 within the replacement period. His business partner, Diaz, whose identical warehouse is also destroyed (same insurance and basis), purchases a replacement for $520,000. How does the reinvestment amount affect recognized gain differently for each taxpayer, and what is the policy rationale for the Section 1033 deferral?',
  choices = '["Henderson recognizes $20,000 gain because he reinvested only $480,000 of the $500,000 proceeds — the $20,000 not reinvested represents the portion of gain the taxpayer chose to extract rather than reinvest. Diaz recognizes $0 gain because the $520,000 reinvestment exceeds the $500,000 proceeds, deferring the entire $150,000 gain into a lower replacement basis ($370,000). Section 1033 exists because involuntary conversions are forced events, and taxing the full gain would penalize taxpayers compelled to replace essential property through no fault of their own", "Henderson recognizes $150,000 and Diaz recognizes $0, because any shortfall in reinvestment triggers full gain recognition", "Both recognize $0 because Section 1033 defers all gain whenever the taxpayer purchases replacement property, regardless of the amount reinvested", "Henderson recognizes $0 and Diaz recognizes $20,000, because excess reinvestment over proceeds creates a taxable event"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under Section 1033, gain from an involuntary conversion is recognized only to the extent proceeds exceed the cost of qualifying replacement property. Henderson: $500,000 proceeds − $480,000 reinvestment = $20,000 recognized. The remaining $130,000 of the $150,000 gain is deferred. Henderson''s replacement basis is $350,000 ($480,000 cost − $130,000 deferred gain). Diaz: $520,000 reinvestment exceeds $500,000 proceeds, so $0 is recognized and the full $150,000 gain is deferred. Diaz''s replacement basis is $370,000 ($520,000 − $150,000). The policy rationale is that involuntary conversions are compelled events — the taxpayer did not choose to realize gain. Requiring full reinvestment for complete deferral ensures the benefit goes to taxpayers who genuinely replace the property rather than pocketing proceeds. (B) is wrong because gain recognition is limited to the shortfall amount, not the full gain. (C) is wrong because deferral requires reinvestment at or above the proceeds amount; partial reinvestment triggers partial recognition. (D) reverses the treatment — excess reinvestment produces no gain; it is shortfalls that trigger recognition.'
WHERE id = 533;

