-- ID 250: Professional Ethics in Tax — Unreported income discovery response analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'CPA Martinez discovers during return preparation that a client has $80,000 in unreported income from a foreign bank account, potentially triggering FBAR and FATCA obligations. The client asks Martinez to "just skip it" and file without the foreign income. How does Martinez''s ethical obligation under AICPA standards and Circular 230 dictate the response, and how does this differ from what would happen if Martinez simply suspected (but could not confirm) unreported income?',
  choices = '["Martinez must advise the client of the specific disclosure obligations (FBAR, FATCA, Form 8938) and the severe penalties for noncompliance (willful FBAR penalties can reach $100,000 or 50% of account balance per violation). If the client refuses to report, Martinez must consider whether to withdraw from the engagement — signing a return known to be materially incomplete violates both AICPA standards and Circular 230. If Martinez merely suspected unreported income without confirmation, the obligation would be to make reasonable inquiries rather than refuse to file", "Martinez should report the client directly to FinCEN because CPAs have a mandatory reporting obligation for suspected unreported foreign income", "Martinez should file the return as requested because the client''s instructions override the CPA''s judgment on reporting decisions", "Martinez should withdraw immediately without advising the client of any obligations, because further communication could constitute aiding noncompliance"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under AICPA Statement on Standards for Tax Services No. 6 and Circular 230 Section 10.21, when a practitioner has actual knowledge of a client''s noncompliance, the practitioner must promptly advise the client of the obligation and consequences. For foreign accounts, this includes FBAR filing (FinCEN 114), FATCA reporting (Form 8938), and the associated penalties. If the client refuses to comply, the CPA cannot sign a return known to omit material income — doing so would violate the preparer''s duty under Section 6694 and Circular 230. The standard differs when the practitioner merely suspects unreported income: reasonable inquiry is required, but the practitioner may rely on client representations if there is no reason to doubt them. (B) is wrong because CPAs do not have a mandatory reporting obligation to FinCEN — their duty is to advise the client. (C) is wrong because a CPA cannot follow client instructions that would result in preparing a materially false return. (D) is wrong because the ethical obligation requires advising the client of noncompliance before considering withdrawal.'
WHERE id = 250;

-- ID 253: Professional Ethics in Tax — Prior-year error response analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'CPA Reeves discovers that a client''s prior-year return contains a $50,000 error that materially understates tax liability. The client acknowledges the error but refuses to file an amended return. Reeves is now preparing the client''s current-year return. How does the AICPA standard govern Reeves''s obligations regarding both the prior-year error and the current-year engagement?',
  choices = '["Reeves must consider whether to continue the engagement and must not sign the current-year return if it is affected by the prior-year error (for example, if an incorrect carryforward flows into the current year). Reeves cannot file an amended return without client authorization because the client controls the decision to amend. However, Reeves must evaluate whether the relationship can continue — if the client''s refusal to correct demonstrates a pattern of dishonesty that undermines the practitioner''s ability to rely on client representations, withdrawal may be necessary", "Reeves must file an amended return on behalf of the client without consent because the CPA''s duty to the tax system overrides client authorization requirements", "Reeves should do nothing because prior-year returns are finalized once filed and the CPA has no continuing obligation", "Reeves must report the client to the IRS because knowing about an understatement creates a mandatory disclosure obligation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under AICPA SSTS No. 6, when a CPA discovers a material error on a prior-year return, the CPA must advise the client to correct it. However, the decision to file an amended return belongs to the client — the CPA cannot do so without authorization. If the client refuses, the CPA must evaluate the impact on the current engagement. If the error affects the current-year return (through carryforwards, basis calculations, or other connections), the CPA cannot sign a return that perpetuates the error. The CPA must also assess whether the client''s refusal to correct a known material error indicates a level of dishonesty that makes continuing the relationship untenable. (B) is wrong because the CPA has no authority to file an amended return without the client''s consent — client autonomy over filing decisions is fundamental. (C) is wrong because the CPA has an ongoing obligation to evaluate how prior errors affect current work. (D) is wrong because CPAs generally have no obligation to report clients to the IRS (except in limited circumstances involving their own potential liability) — the duty is to advise the client.'
WHERE id = 253;

-- ID 2405: Professional Ethics in Tax — Circular 230 noncompliance notification analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Practitioner Fowler learns during a meeting that her client failed to report $120,000 of cryptocurrency gains on last year''s return. A different practitioner, Grant, suspects a client may have unreported income but has no concrete evidence. How does Circular 230 Section 10.21 apply differently to each practitioner, and what distinguishes the obligation when a practitioner has actual knowledge versus mere suspicion?',
  choices = '["Fowler must promptly advise the client of the noncompliance ($120,000 unreported crypto gains) and its consequences (penalties, interest, potential fraud exposure). Grant, who merely suspects unreported income, should make reasonable inquiries but is not required to take affirmative action based on unconfirmed suspicion. Section 10.21 triggers a duty to advise only when the practitioner knows of noncompliance — this knowledge-based standard prevents practitioners from ignoring confirmed problems while protecting them from liability for unverified concerns", "Both practitioners must immediately report their clients to the IRS Criminal Investigation Division", "Fowler should file an amended return without consulting the client to correct the noncompliance", "Neither practitioner has any obligation because Circular 230 only applies to written tax advice, not oral communications about prior returns"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Circular 230 Section 10.21 states that a practitioner who knows a client has not complied with revenue laws must promptly advise the client of the noncompliance and its consequences. The word "knows" is critical — Fowler has confirmed knowledge of the $120,000 unreported cryptocurrency gains, triggering the affirmative duty to advise immediately. Grant''s mere suspicion does not trigger the same mandatory obligation, though professional standards encourage reasonable inquiry. The knowledge-based threshold balances two competing interests: ensuring practitioners address confirmed noncompliance while not imposing a duty to investigate every uncertainty. Note that the duty is to advise the client, not to report to the IRS — the practitioner is not an informant. (B) is wrong because practitioners have no obligation to report clients to IRS criminal investigation — their duty is to advise the client. (C) is wrong because the practitioner cannot file an amended return without client authorization. (D) is wrong because Section 10.21 applies to all forms of communication and all types of noncompliance, not just written tax advice.'
WHERE id = 2405;

