-- ID 1176: Debtor-Creditor Relationships — Nondischargeable debt policy analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Davis files Chapter 7 bankruptcy with $200,000 in credit card debt, $80,000 in medical bills, and $150,000 in student loans. After the bankruptcy discharge, which debts are eliminated and which survive? How does the nondischargeability of student loans reflect a policy choice about the nature of educational investment?',
  choices = '["The credit card debt and medical bills are discharged because they represent typical consumer obligations that bankruptcy is designed to relieve. The student loans survive the discharge absent a showing of undue hardship — Congress made student loans nondischargeable because education is an intangible asset that cannot be repossessed, creating a moral hazard where borrowers could obtain degrees and immediately discharge the debt before earning returns on the education", "All three debts are discharged because Chapter 7 eliminates all unsecured debts without exception", "Only the medical bills are discharged; both credit card debt and student loans survive because they were voluntarily incurred", "Student loans are discharged first because they are the highest priority unsecured debt in bankruptcy"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Chapter 7 discharge eliminates most unsecured debts including credit card balances and medical bills, giving the debtor a fresh start. Student loans are an explicit exception under Section 523(a)(8) — they survive discharge unless the debtor demonstrates undue hardship through an adversary proceeding (typically using the Brunner test). The policy rationale is unique to education: unlike a car or house, a degree cannot be repossessed by the lender. If student loans were freely dischargeable, rational borrowers could obtain expensive degrees, discharge the loans immediately, and retain the lifetime earning benefit — undermining the lending system that finances higher education. This moral hazard concern, combined with the government''s role as guarantor of most student loans, drove Congress to create the exception. (B) is wrong because bankruptcy law contains numerous exceptions to discharge, including student loans, recent taxes, child support, and fraud-related debts. (C) is wrong because credit card debt is generally dischargeable; the voluntary nature of the debt is not the distinguishing factor. (D) is wrong because student loans are among the hardest debts to discharge, not the easiest.'
WHERE id = 1176;

-- ID 1178: Debtor-Creditor Relationships — Automatic stay scope and limitation analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Upon filing Chapter 7, Yamamoto''s mortgage lender halts foreclosure proceedings, and a credit card company stops calling. However, the IRS continues a criminal tax fraud investigation. How does the automatic stay affect each of these creditor actions differently, and what is the consequence if a creditor violates the stay?',
  choices = '["The automatic stay halts the foreclosure and collection calls because it immediately enjoins most civil collection actions upon filing. The IRS criminal investigation continues because the stay does not apply to criminal proceedings — this exception prevents debtors from using bankruptcy to obstruct criminal law enforcement. A creditor who violates the stay (such as continuing to call despite the filing) may face contempt sanctions, actual damages, and attorneys'' fees awarded to the debtor", "The automatic stay stops all actions against the debtor including criminal proceedings, because bankruptcy protection is absolute", "The automatic stay only applies to secured creditors, so the credit card company can continue calling while the foreclosure is halted", "The automatic stay takes effect 30 days after filing, giving creditors time to complete pending actions"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The automatic stay under Section 362 is one of bankruptcy''s most powerful protections, taking effect immediately upon filing. It halts virtually all civil collection actions: foreclosures, lawsuits, garnishments, repossessions, and collection communications. However, Section 362(b) contains specific exceptions, including criminal proceedings against the debtor — the IRS criminal investigation continues unaffected because allowing debtors to block criminal enforcement through bankruptcy would undermine the justice system. The consequence for stay violations is significant: under Section 362(k), an individual debtor injured by a willful violation may recover actual damages (including attorneys'' fees) and, in appropriate cases, punitive damages. This enforcement mechanism gives the stay real teeth. (B) is wrong because the automatic stay explicitly exempts criminal proceedings and several other categories. (C) is wrong because the stay applies to both secured and unsecured creditors — all civil collection activity is halted. (D) is wrong because the stay takes effect immediately upon filing, not after a waiting period.'
WHERE id = 1178;

-- ID 1180: Debtor-Creditor Relationships — Perfection priority analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'First Bank perfects a security interest in Meridian Corp''s equipment by filing a financing statement on March 1. Second Bank takes a security interest in the same equipment on February 15 but does not file until March 15. A buyer in the ordinary course of business purchases one piece of equipment on March 10. How does perfection timing establish priority among these competing claims, and what different rule applies to the buyer?',
  choices = '["First Bank has priority over Second Bank because the first to file or perfect prevails under UCC Article 9''s priority rules — First Bank filed March 1 while Second Bank filed March 15, even though Second Bank''s security interest attached earlier. The buyer takes the equipment free of both security interests because UCC Section 9-320 protects buyers in the ordinary course of business from perfected security interests, ensuring that commercial transactions are not paralyzed by hidden liens on inventory or equipment sold in the normal course", "Second Bank has priority because its security interest attached first on February 15, regardless of when it was filed", "First Bank has priority over everyone, including the buyer, because filing a financing statement creates an absolute lien that no subsequent party can defeat", "The buyer must check for financing statements before purchasing, and failure to do so means both banks have priority over the buyer"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): UCC Article 9 uses a first-to-file-or-perfect rule for priority among competing security interests. First Bank filed March 1; Second Bank filed March 15. Even though Second Bank''s interest attached earlier (February 15), attachment alone does not establish priority — filing does. First Bank wins the priority contest. The buyer in the ordinary course of business is protected by a separate rule under Section 9-320(a), which allows buyers who purchase goods in the ordinary course of the seller''s business to take free of security interests created by the seller, even perfected ones. This rule is essential for commercial fluidity — without it, every retail or wholesale purchaser would need to search financing statement records before buying, making commerce impractical. (B) is wrong because the priority rule is first to file or perfect, not first to attach. (C) is wrong because the buyer in ordinary course exception specifically defeats perfected security interests. (D) is wrong because buyers in the ordinary course have no duty to search financing statements — the UCC protects them automatically.'
WHERE id = 1180;

